  "id": "COMPOUND:34435 - PROTEIN:731",
  "verbalization": "Ent-Gloeosteretriol appears to interact with Activated CDC42 kinase 1 through a series of molecular pathways, with the latter being the target of multiple compounds that are themselves targets of Ent-Gloeosteretriol. The evidence suggests that Ent-Gloeosteretriol may modulate the activity of a serine/threonine-protein kinase (ICK) or a phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 3-kinase C2 domain-containing subunit (C2 domain), both of which are implicated in signaling pathways that ultimately lead to the activation of CDC42 kinase 1. This implies that Ent-Gloeosteretriol could influence cellular processes by affecting the activity of these upstream regulators, thereby contributing to the activation of the CDC42 kinase 1. The consistent involvement of Activated CDC42 kinase 1 across different targets suggests a potential role in downstream signaling events, possibly in cell proliferation or other cellular functions. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of Ent-Gloeosteretriol in orchestrating a network of interactions that culminate in the activation of CDC42 kinase 1.",
